<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        WHAT A PLAN IS MADE OF ON SCREEN - one step, one sentence, one command, and the way out of
        the panel. Templates and styles only, not one value.

        THE TWELFTH THEME FILE, ON 2026-09-02, AND THE MARKUP RATCHET IS WHAT ASKED. The owner
        looked at the built panel and said it is ugly. PlanView.xaml stood EXACTLY on the 376-line
        ceiling, so the redesign could not add a line to it until something came out - which is the
        ratchet doing the job SizeCeilings describes: a file at the ceiling makes the next person
        look for a seam before they look for room.

        THE SEAM IS THE ONE THIS PRODUCT KEEPS FINDING: what a thing IS stays in the view, what a
        thing LOOKS LIKE comes here. Nothing moved that binds a Click - `docs/10` and AppearanceGuards
        both record why, and it is not a style choice: a resource dictionary has no code-behind, so
        a handler named in markup cannot be resolved from one. The close button below is the
        borderline case and it passes, because a style carries no handler - PlanView.xaml still owns
        the Click and hands this the look.

        MERGED AFTER Controls.xaml, which is not optional. The close button reaches
        PrimaryActionFocusRing through StaticResource, and StaticResource resolves WHILE THIS FILE
        IS BEING READ - so a dictionary merged after this one is a name that does not exist yet.
        App.xaml carries the order and three other places repeat it: WpfHost in the tests,
        tools/wpfui-probe, and the pool in AppearanceGuards.
    -->

    <!--
        ONE STEP. The number is part of the sentence rather than a column, because a plan is read
        as prose - "3. stop Netlogon (would break otherwise)" - and a person counting steps wants
        to see where they are in the order.

        WHAT SOMEBODY ASKED FOR READS DIFFERENTLY FROM WHAT COMES ALONG, and `C2` is why: the
        plan warns that stopping three drags seven along, so the seven have to be findable at a
        glance. Subdued goes to the ones nobody asked for, which is the right way round.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="PlanLineTemplate">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding Text}"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
            <TextBlock.Style>
                <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource PlanStepText}">
                    <Style.Triggers>
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Asked}" Value="True">
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                    </Style.Triggers>
                </Style>
            </TextBlock.Style>
        </TextBlock>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        ONE SENTENCE IN A LIST OF THEM: a warning, a refusal, or something that did not work.

        IT NAMED NO COLOUR UNTIL 2026-09-02 AND WAS THEREFORE DRAWN IN SYSTEM BLACK. The implicit
        TextBlock style sets a face and a size and no Foreground, so a TextBlock that names neither
        gets the framework's default - which is black. On this panel's #343434 that is a contrast
        ratio of **1.69**, against the 4.5 WCAG asks. The owner's words were that the panel is black
        text on grey and that nothing is visible in it, and a scan of the capture agreed exactly:
        in the band holding this sentence NOT ONE PIXEL was brighter than the background.

        MY OWN EYE READ THE SAME PICTURE AS LIGHT GREY, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS WRITTEN DOWN. The
        glyphs are black and their antialiased edges are not, so at a glance the mass reads as grey
        - `docs/11` already records that at these ratios you cannot tell "the colour is not
        arriving" from "the colour arrives and nobody sees it". The instrument settles it, not the
        eye, and the same fault is recorded beside HeadingText in Themes/Text.xaml from 2026-09-01.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="PlanSentenceTemplate">
        <TextBlock Text="{Binding}"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Foreground="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}"
                   Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}" />
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        A COMMAND LINE, IN THE FIXED WIDTH FACE. `docs/03` part 4 asks for it wherever two
        different things must not be able to look the same, and a command somebody is about to
        paste into a terminal is exactly that - one wrong character is a different service.

        THE FACE IS ASKED FOR BY STYLE RATHER THAN BY SETTER SINCE 2026-09-02, and that is the same
        repair as the sentence above rather than tidying: this named the face and not the colour, so
        the one thing on the panel a person is meant to COPY was drawn in system black at 1.69
        against the surface under it. FixedText names both, and has named both since the day another
        style was caught with the same hole.
    -->
    <!--
        THE RECESS A COMMAND SITS IN. Darker than the sheet, one point of edge, and padded on both
        sides - the argument for the fill is beside SurfaceCommandBox in Colours.xaml.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanCommandBox" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceCommandBox}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource CornerTile}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadChip}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginChip}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE BUTTON THAT TAKES IT AWAY. Quiet on purpose: it stands beside a command in a fixed face,
        and a filled button there would be the loudest thing in a block whose subject is the text.
        An outline says "control" without saying "press me before you read this".

        IT KEEPS ITS SIZE WHEN THE WORD CHANGES. Copy becomes Copied for two seconds, and a box that
        grew by two characters would shift the command beside it every time somebody pressed it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanCopyButton" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadChip}" />
        <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="{StaticResource WidthCopyButton}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <Border x:Name="Face"
                            Background="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource SurfaceChipEdge}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHover}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        AND SINCE 2026-09-02 IT HAS A WAY OFF THE SCREEN, WHICH IS THE OWNER'S FIRST REMARK OF THE
        SECOND SERIES. The command was a sentence somebody had to retype by hand, one character at
        a time, into a terminal where one wrong character is a different service - the exact risk
        the fixed-width face was chosen to reduce, left standing in the one place it mattered most.

        A BOX PER COMMAND RATHER THAN ONE BUTTON FOR THE BLOCK. A plan over several picked rows
        prints one line each, and copying all of them at once is a different thing from copying the
        one somebody is about to run.

        THE BUTTON IS ALWAYS THERE RATHER THAN ON HOVER. A control that appears under the pointer is
        prettier and invisible to anybody working from the keyboard, and it is invisible to anybody
        who does not already know it is there - which is everybody, the first time.

        IT STOPPED WRAPPING AND STARTED SCROLLING. A long command used to grow the sheet downwards
        and move the button under it, which is how a person aims at one thing and presses another.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="PlanCommandTemplate">
        <Border Style="{StaticResource PlanCommandBox}">
            <Grid>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <ScrollViewer Grid.Column="0"
                              HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                              VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" Style="{StaticResource FixedText}" />
                </ScrollViewer>

                <!--
                    THE COMMAND TRAVELS ON THE BUTTON ITSELF, in Tag, because an ItemsControl builds
                    one of these per line and whoever handles the press has to know WHICH. Reading
                    the selection, or the DataContext of whatever had focus, is how the wrong line
                    gets copied.

                    AND THERE IS NO Click HERE, WHICH IS PULAPKA 10 ARRIVING IN A NEW SHAPE. A theme
                    file is a plain dictionary with no code behind it, so a handler named here would
                    not resolve against anything. The press is caught by PlanView.xaml instead, on
                    the ItemsControl above this template, where Click arrives by bubbling and the
                    markup is compiled with a class that can hold the method.
                -->
                <Button Grid.Column="1"
                        Style="{StaticResource PlanCopyButton}"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planCopyCommand"
                        AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.plan.copy}"
                        Content="{DynamicResource gui.plan.copy}"
                        ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.plan.copy.hint}"
                        Tag="{Binding}" />
            </Grid>
        </Border>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        THE WAY OUT OF THE PANEL, AND IT BECAME A MARK RATHER THAN A WORD ON 2026-09-02 FOR ONE
        MEASURED REASON: it was standing on the title's line and taking the room the title needed.

        Read off the owner's capture of the built window. The panel is 380 wide, its padding takes
        28 and the title's own group margin 20, which leaves 332 - and the library's Close button
        was about 66 of it. The title left with 266 could not fit "AssignedAccessManager" beside
        the words around it, so a two-line title became a THREE-line title, a hundred pixels of a
        panel 380 tall. Giving those 66 back is the whole of this change: the cross is about 28
        across, so the title gets roughly 38 points and its third line back.

        AND IT STOPS COMPETING, WHICH IS THE SECOND HALF. A filled, bordered rectangle carrying a
        word was the heaviest object in the panel after the title, sitting beside it. What closes a
        panel is furniture rather than an action - the actions here are Carry this out and
        Interrupt - so it wears no fill until a pointer is on it.

        THE MARK IS A Path AND NOT A CHARACTER, the same decision as the disclosure in Chips.xaml
        and for the same two measured reasons: a glyph needs a font that carries it on Windows
        Server, and a TextBlock in CONTENT gets an automation node of its own. A Shape gets no
        peer, so the tree keeps one node for one button - and PlanView.xaml sets
        AutomationProperties.Name on it, because a button with no text has no name without one.

        THE RING IS THE PRIMARY ACTION'S, BORROWED BY NAME. It is a rounded rectangle the size of
        whatever it is drawn on, so nothing about it is specific to that button - and `ADR-23`
        prefers one ring named once to a second one that will drift from it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanClose" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{StaticResource PrimaryActionFocusRing}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <!--
                        Transparent rather than no brush at all, which is `docs/10` trap 11: a
                        transparent brush TAKES the pointer and a missing one does not, so without
                        this the button would only be pressable on the two strokes of the cross.
                    -->
                    <Border x:Name="Face"
                            Background="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <Path Data="M 0,0 L 10,10 M 10,0 L 0,10"
                              Stroke="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                              StrokeThickness="1.3"
                              StrokeStartLineCap="Round"
                              StrokeEndLineCap="Round" />
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHover}" />
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        A BLOCK WITH A LINE DOWN ITS LEFT SIDE, WHICH IS THE ONE NEW IDEA IN THE PANEL'S REDESIGN.

        WHAT WAS WRONG. Every section of the panel was a heading with sentences under it and nothing
        else - six of them in a column, told apart only by their words. The owner's capture shows the
        result: three labels of equal weight down a narrow column, and no way to see which block is
        WHAT WILL HAPPEN and which is a note about it. There was nothing to look at, only things to
        read.

        WHAT THE RULE SAYS AND WHY IT IS CHEAP. A block that matters gets a line beside it and the
        COLOUR OF THE LINE SAYS WHICH KIND. No fill, so nothing new has to clear a contrast ratio
        against the panel, and no corner, no shadow, no second surface. Two blocks carry one, which
        is the whole list on purpose: the steps, because they are what the machine would do, and the
        line saying why the button underneath will refuse. A rule on all six would be furniture
        again.

        NOT ONE NEW VALUE, AND THAT IS AN ARGUMENT RATHER THAN THRIFT. The rule is BorderPanelEdge,
        the one-point left edge this panel already draws against the list, so every hairline on this
        screen is the same weight - a three-point accent rule would be the only stroke of its
        thickness in the window and would read as a different product.

        THE PADDING WAS PaddingPanel FOR ONE DAY AND IT COST TOO MUCH. That value is 14,12,14,12,
        which with MarginSection's 18 and 4 is 46 points of vertical chrome per block - on a panel
        355 points tall, for two blocks, that is a quarter of it spent on air. Measured on the
        owner's own capture the day after: with the refusal block on screen the body was 24 per cent
        of the panel and the footer was 44. PadCell keeps the eight points of left air the rule
        needs and spends four above and below instead of twelve.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanBlock" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceChipEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderPanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginSection}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The refusal sits in the FOOTER rather than among the sections, so it takes the footer's
        rhythm rather than the section rhythm: a gap under it and none above, because the rule the
        footer already draws is what separates it from the body.
    -->
    <!--
        THE RULE WENT FROM WARNING TO REFUSED ON 2026-09-02, ON THE OWNER'S WORDS. The only sentence
        this block ever holds is the one saying this session has no administrator rights - Planned
        writes nothing else into Blocked - and amber says "something might go wrong" where the truth
        is "nothing here can be done at all". The rule and the words inside it now agree.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanBlockRefused" TargetType="Border" BasedOn="{StaticResource PlanBlock}">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE DIMMING BEHIND THE SHEET, 2026-09-02. It fills whatever it is put in and it EATS THE
        CLICKS, which is the half that is not decoration: while this question is on screen the list
        under it must not be selectable, or somebody answers a plan about a row they have since
        changed. A brush is what makes that true - `docs/10` records that a transparent fill takes
        hit tests and NO fill takes none, so the colour here is load bearing twice over.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanScrim" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceScrim}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE SHEET ITSELF, AND IT REPLACED A SIDE PANEL ON 2026-09-02 ON THE OWNER'S DECISION.

        WHAT THE PANEL DID WRONG WAS NOT ITS LOOK, IT WAS ITS PLACE. Sharing a row with the list
        meant taking width from it, so opening a plan trimmed `Display name` to nothing, cut the
        account column in half and grew a horizontal scrollbar under a list that never needed one.
        At 1000 by 800 it was worse: the panel took its own vertical scrollbar and a section heading
        was left on screen with nothing under it. Every one of those is a width argument, and a
        sheet does not have that argument - it is centred over the window and takes what it needs.

        A PLAN IS A QUESTION, NOT AN INSPECTOR, and that is the reason underneath the geometry. It
        asks whether to change this machine, it is answered once, and until it is answered nothing
        else in the window is worth pressing. A panel says "look at this beside your list". This
        says "answer this". The cost is real and was said out loud before it was chosen: the list
        can no longer be read while the plan is open.

        BORDER, RADIUS AND SHADOW TOGETHER, because two of the three read as a mistake. A fill with
        no edge is a rectangle somebody painted over the window - which is what the panel looked
        like and what the owner called flat. The shadow is what says WHICH layer is on top, and
        without it a centred box just covers things.

        THE MARGIN IS THE MAXIMUM HEIGHT. Centred with air above and below, the sheet can never be
        taller than the window minus that air, and its body scrolls inside itself instead - so the
        buttons at the foot are always on screen. That is the fault this replaces, said as geometry.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PlanSheet" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanel}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource CornerPlanSheet}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PaddingPanel}" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginPlanSheet}" />

        <Setter Property="Effect">
            <Setter.Value>
                <!--
                    Black rather than a colour, and soft rather than tight: a shadow that can be
                    SEEN as a shape reads as a border drawn badly. This one only has to say that
                    there is space between the sheet and the window behind it.
                -->
                <DropShadowEffect Color="Black"
                                  BlurRadius="28"
                                  ShadowDepth="8"
                                  Direction="270"
                                  Opacity="0.55" />
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
